 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family map 3D spatial coordinates $(u, v, w)$ to a linear memory address or buffer index to improve spatial locality [G]. The core mechanism involves decomposing the volume into small, fixed-size blocks (miniblocks, chunks, or tiles) and applying a transformation to the coordinate bits or block indices [G]. Common mathematical operations include:
-1.  **Integer Division:** Mapping voxel coordinates to block/miniblock coordinates using floor division (e.g., $u_m = \lfloor u / 2 \rfloor$) [Voxel Memory Bank Allocation and Miniblock Organization](1638-voxel-memory-bank-allocation-and-miniblock-organization.md).
-2.  **Modulo Arithmetic:** Distributing blocks across memory modules or banks using modulo operations (e.g., $M = (u_m + v_m + w_m) \bmod 4$) to ensure adjacent blocks map to distinct hardware resources [Voxel Memory Bank Allocation and Miniblock Organization](1638-voxel-memory-bank-allocation-and-miniblock-organization.md).
-3.  **Bit Interleaving:** Rearranging the bits of coordinate components to group spatially adjacent voxels in memory, often separating most significant bits (MSBs) from least significant bits (LSBs) [Volume Interleaving](2174-volume-interleaving.md).
-4.  **Padding/Alignment:** Padding volume dimensions to multiples of specific block sizes (e.g., 32) to ensure uniform access patterns and simplify hardware logic [Voxel Memory Bank Allocation and Miniblock Organization](1638-voxel-memory-bank-allocation-and-miniblock-organization.md).
 
